package example_table;

import common.ExampleNotes;

/** Demonstrator notes of GemTableExample (see common.ExampleNotes). */
component GemTableNotes() {
  @ExampleNotes(
    title = "Table",
    docComponents = ["GemTable", "GemTableHeader", "GemTableRow", "GemTableHeaderCell", "GemTableRowCell"],
    about = [
      "What you see.|Two tables. The first lists the persons of the demo database with an editable birthday field in every row. The second has a fixed layout and cells that span rows and columns: a two-row header (\"Name\" spans both rows, \"Address\" spans two columns) and body cells that span two rows.",
      "How it is used.|headers takes GemTableHeader rows, body takes GemTableRow rows; both accept either cols (a plain list of contents) or cells (GemTableHeaderCell / GemTableRowCell components with rowSpan and colSpan). Any component may be a cell - the first table puts a text input into each row, bound to p.birthday, so the table edits the database."
    ],
    tryIt = [
      "Change a birthday in the first table, then open a chart example: the age groups of the charts are computed from these dates.",
      "Open \"Show GUI\": the first table is an @iterate over the persons, the second is written cell by cell.",
      "Open \"Parameters\" of GemTable: layout = \"fixed\" spreads the columns evenly instead of fitting the content."
    ]
  );
}
